1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:07,509
On the evening of October sixth, twenty twenty-six, OpenAI quietly posted a folder to the internet.

2
00:00:07,559 --> 00:00:14,254
Inside were seven hundred and twenty-two mathematical manuscripts, written by an artificial intelligence.

3
00:00:14,304 --> 00:00:17,016
Some mathematicians called it jaw-dropping.

4
00:00:17,066 --> 00:00:19,714
Others called it a demonstration of power.

5
00:00:19,764 --> 00:00:24,661
This is a short guide to what happened, what was claimed, and why it matters.

6
00:00:27,011 --> 00:00:28,513
Here is the shape of it.

7
00:00:28,563 --> 00:00:34,075
OpenAI gave an unreleased internal model roughly four thousand open research problems.

8
00:00:34,125 --> 00:00:34,916
Not homework.

9
00:00:34,966 --> 00:00:36,210
Not contest puzzles.

10
00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:41,578
Questions that human experts had tried and failed to settle, sometimes for decades.

11
00:00:41,628 --> 00:00:52,056
The model's successful outputs were sorted into three hundred seventy-two families of related results, and written up as seven hundred and twenty-two manuscripts.

12
00:00:52,106 --> 00:01:00,206
OpenAI says each result cost, on average, about three hours of the heaviest thinking compute that its ChatGPT Pro tier offers.

13
00:01:00,256 --> 00:01:05,056
The repository is public, free to read, and released under an open license.

14
00:01:07,406 --> 00:01:11,317
The obvious question is whether any of this is actually correct.

15
00:01:11,367 --> 00:01:15,648
A proof written by a machine could be nonsense dressed up in notation.

16
00:01:15,698 --> 00:01:18,247
That is where a tool called Lean comes in.

17
00:01:18,297 --> 00:01:24,867
Lean is a proof assistant: a program that will only accept a proof if every single logical step checks out.

18
00:01:24,917 --> 00:01:28,828
As the mathematician Kevin Buzzard put it, you cannot fool Lean.

19
00:01:28,878 --> 00:01:33,592
About forty-two percent of the headline results come with a Lean certificate.

20
00:01:33,642 --> 00:01:36,438
For those, the logic has been machine-checked.

21
00:01:36,488 --> 00:01:42,317
The rest have not been formalized, and OpenAI itself warns that some of them could have issues.

22
00:01:42,367 --> 00:01:43,369
One more wrinkle.

23
00:01:43,419 --> 00:01:48,814
In a few cases, what Lean verified is a weaker statement than the headline in the paper.

24
00:01:48,864 --> 00:01:52,897
So a green checkmark is not always a checkmark on the whole claim.

25
00:01:55,247 --> 00:01:57,898
Now to the result everyone is talking about.

26
00:01:57,948 --> 00:02:06,122
The Riemann Hypothesis is a hundred-and-sixty-seven-year-old question about prime numbers: two, three, five, seven, eleven, and so on.

27
00:02:06,172 --> 00:02:08,393
Primes look random, but they are not.

28
00:02:08,443 --> 00:02:18,029
There is a hidden rhythm to how they thin out along the number line, and that rhythm is controlled by a mathematical object called the Riemann zeta function.

29
00:02:18,079 --> 00:02:21,098
The zeta function has special points called zeros.

30
00:02:21,148 --> 00:02:26,867
Riemann guessed in eighteen fifty-nine that every one of those zeros sits exactly on one line.

31
00:02:26,917 --> 00:02:30,488
If he was right, we understand the primes almost perfectly.

32
00:02:30,538 --> 00:02:35,337
It is one of the seven Millennium Prize problems, with a million dollar bounty.

33
00:02:37,687 --> 00:02:39,227
Picture a vertical strip.

34
00:02:39,277 --> 00:02:43,234
Riemann said all the zeros lie on the center line, at one half.

35
00:02:43,284 --> 00:02:49,913
For over a century, the best anyone could prove was much weaker: no zeros at the right-hand edge, at one.

36
00:02:49,963 --> 00:02:52,712
Everything in between was unknown territory.

37
00:02:52,762 --> 00:02:58,182
OpenAI's model claims to have proved there are no zeros to the right of seven-eighths.

38
00:02:58,232 --> 00:03:02,316
A second manuscript pushes a related argument to eleven-twelfths.

39
00:03:02,366 --> 00:03:04,606
That is nowhere near Riemann's line.

40
00:03:04,656 --> 00:03:10,776
But it is the first time that boundary has moved in a meaningful way since the eighteen hundreds.

41
00:03:10,826 --> 00:03:21,144
Hector Pasten, a number theorist in Chile, told Scientific American that the quasi-Riemann hypothesis is, in his words, way more than enough for many applications.

42
00:03:21,194 --> 00:03:29,031
Online, analytic number theorists debated whether this ranks above or below the Prime Number Theorem of eighteen ninety-six.

43
00:03:29,081 --> 00:03:32,466
Either way, if it holds, it is Fields Medal territory.

44
00:03:34,816 --> 00:03:38,734
And Riemann is only one family out of three hundred seventy-two.

45
00:03:38,784 --> 00:03:46,360
In computer science, the model claims a faster way to multiply matrices, the operation that powers almost all of modern AI.

46
00:03:46,410 --> 00:03:51,321
One researcher joked that the language models are trying to speed themselves up.

47
00:03:51,371 --> 00:04:01,613
In number theory, it extended Hilbert's tenth problem, settled a famous question about Siegel zeros, and proved a conjecture of Paul Erdős on arithmetic progressions.

48
00:04:01,663 --> 00:04:10,541
In geometry, it claims the four-dimensional Kakeya conjecture, one dimension beyond the result that earned Hong Wang a Fields Medal this summer.

49
00:04:10,591 --> 00:04:22,570
In physics, it claims to solve a warm-up version of the Yang-Mills mass gap problem, which a Harvard physicist called groundbreaking and comprehensible, while calling a companion paper horrible.

50
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:24,802
Some of these will survive scrutiny.

51
00:04:24,852 --> 00:04:25,608
Some may not.

52
00:04:25,658 --> 00:04:30,134
Several have already been retracted after outside experts found mistakes.

53
00:04:32,484 --> 00:04:36,301
So why are many mathematicians angry rather than celebrating?

54
00:04:36,351 --> 00:04:46,824
In September, twenty-five Fields Medalists, including Terence Tao, signed an open letter saying the goals of AI companies and of mathematics were severely misaligned.

55
00:04:46,874 --> 00:04:52,212
Solving famous problems for publicity, they argued, is not the same as understanding.

56
00:04:52,262 --> 00:05:00,073
The day after the release, a group calling itself the Association for Human Mathematics published a statement on Tao's blog.

57
00:05:00,123 --> 00:05:03,940
Mathematicians did not ask for this work to be done, it said.

58
00:05:03,990 --> 00:05:09,454
Releasing seven hundred files at once is not scholarship, but a demonstration of power.

59
00:05:09,504 --> 00:05:12,751
It urged mathematicians to stop working with OpenAI.

60
00:05:12,801 --> 00:05:15,223
Notice what the statement does not say.

61
00:05:15,273 --> 00:05:17,632
It does not claim any result is wrong.

62
00:05:17,682 --> 00:05:24,224
The fight is about process, credit, and who gets to set the pace of a four-thousand-year-old discipline.

63
00:05:26,574 --> 00:05:28,122
Others see it differently.

64
00:05:28,172 --> 00:05:41,025
Kevin Buzzard, who leads a major effort to formalize mathematics, argued before the release that OpenAI should simply dump its theorems into the open, and that keeping them secret would be closer to censorship.

65
00:05:41,075 --> 00:05:49,442
Experts quoted in Scientific American called the batch jaw-dropping, and said some results could have won top research prizes a year ago.

66
00:05:49,492 --> 00:05:51,347
And there is a practical point.

67
00:05:51,397 --> 00:05:52,699
The proofs are public.

68
00:05:52,749 --> 00:05:55,894
Anyone can read them, check them, and build on them.

69
00:05:55,944 --> 00:06:05,540
Within days, outsiders had re-run the Lean check on the quasi-Riemann result, and one group extended the matrix multiplication bound further than OpenAI did.

70
00:06:07,890 --> 00:06:10,598
Why should anyone outside mathematics care?

71
00:06:10,648 --> 00:06:16,306
Because mathematics is the cleanest possible test of whether a machine can really reason.

72
00:06:16,356 --> 00:06:17,396
There is no spin.

73
00:06:17,446 --> 00:06:21,949
A proof is either right or it is wrong, and a computer can check which.

74
00:06:21,999 --> 00:06:32,851
This release suggests that a single AI system, running one fixed procedure for a few hours, can now produce research-level results across twenty different fields at once.

75
00:06:32,901 --> 00:06:34,583
The bottleneck has flipped.

76
00:06:34,633 --> 00:06:37,020
Generating candidate answers is cheap.

77
00:06:37,070 --> 00:06:41,830
Reading, checking, and understanding them is now the scarce human resource.

78
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:45,998
And the hardest questions turn out not to be mathematical at all.

79
00:06:46,048 --> 00:06:47,024
Who gets credit?

80
00:06:47,074 --> 00:06:49,910
How fast should a field be allowed to change?

81
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:56,002
And who benefits when a private company can do, overnight, what a community did over a century?

82
00:06:58,352 --> 00:07:00,548
What happens next will take months.

83
00:07:00,598 --> 00:07:04,847
Mathematicians are working through the repository family by family.

84
00:07:04,897 --> 00:07:06,772
Some claims will be confirmed.

85
00:07:06,822 --> 00:07:08,761
Some will be quietly withdrawn.

86
00:07:08,811 --> 00:07:17,809
The Advisory Group on Mathematics and A.I., hosted at the Institute for Advanced Study, is trying to write rules for how this should be done.

87
00:07:17,859 --> 00:07:19,605
For now, one thing is clear.

88
00:07:19,655 --> 00:07:24,033
A boundary that had not moved since the eighteen hundreds just moved.

89
00:07:24,083 --> 00:07:27,819
The argument over who moved it, and how, is only beginning.

90
00:07:30,169 --> 00:07:31,890
Sources are listed here.

91
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:33,367
Thanks for watching.
